    <solution><p>SOLUTION</p>
<p><me>f'(x) = 3x^{2}-12x-63 = 3(x-7)(x+3)</me>
The critical numbers are <m>x = 7</m> and <m>x = -3</m>.</p>
<p>(a) Only the critical number <m>x = -3</m> is in the interval <m>[-4, 0]</m>.
The value of the function at this critical number is
<me>f(-3) = 120</me>
The values at the endpoints are 
<me>f(-4) = 104 \quad \text{and} \quad f(0)=12</me>
Comparing these values we see that:</p>
<p><B> 1. </B>   Absolute maximum = <m>120</m></p>
<p><B> 2. </B> Absolute minimum = <m>12</m>.</p>
<p>(b) Only the critical number <m>x = 7</m> is in the interval <m>[-1, 8]</m>.
The value of the function at this critical number is
<me>f(7) = -380</me>
The values at the endpoints are 
<me>f(-1) = 68 \quad \text{and} \quad f(8)=-364</me>
Comparing these values we see that:</p>
<p><B> 1. </B>   Absolute maximum = <m>68</m></p>
<p><B> 2. </B> Absolute minimum = <m>-380</m>.</p>
<p>(c) Both  critical numbers <m>x = -3</m> and <m>x = 7</m> are in the interval <m>[-4, 8]</m>.
The values of the function at these critical numbers are
<me>f(-3) = 120 \quad \text{and} \quad f(7) = -380</me>
The values at the endpoints are 
<me>f(-4) = 104 \quad \text{and} \quad f(8)=-364</me>
Comparing these values we see that:</p>
<p><B> 1. </B>   Absolute maximum = <m>120</m></p>
<p><B> 2. </B> Absolute minimum = <m>-380</m>.</p>
</solution>
